INT. FERRY CAFETERIA - NIGHT CROSSING

Orange plastic booths. A coffee urn rattles with the engine. Rain on the windows. Nobody else aboard.

DOLORES (60s, black cardigan, rosary wound around her wrist) sits stiff. Across from her, PAZ (40s, leather jacket, same jaw) peels a label off a water bottle.

HENRIK (50s, rumpled suit, folder under his arm) arrives with two paper cups. Sets one down. Hesitates. Sets the other down too.

She doesn't touch hers. She watches her sister.

HENRIK
Your mother's will is short.

DOLORES
Mine, she says. My mother. Not yours.

Paz stops peeling. She looks up.

PAZ
Our mother.

She slides the label across the table. A small, damp scrap.

HENRIK
It leaves the house to one of you.

She laughs. Short. Ugly. The other stays silent.

DOLORES
Which one.

HENRIK
The one who stayed.

Paz stands. Sits again. Her knee bounces under the table.

PAZ
Twenty years she called me every Sunday.

DOLORES
And I washed her every morning.

She slams a palm on the Formica. The urn rattles louder. Henrik flinches, opens the folder, closes it.

HENRIK
There's a letter. She asked me to read it only if you both were present.

DOLORES
Read it, then.

He unfolds the page. Clears his throat. Her eyes are already wet. The other's are not.

HENRIK
(reading)
"Neither of you gets the house."

Silence. The ferry horn sounds, long and low.

She reaches for the cup. Her hands shake. Paz takes the other cup, drinks, and nods once at the window.

PAZ
She never could make a decision.

DOLORES
She made this one.
